e brain as an organ  when we see somebody's behavior we think  that they have the capacity to change  but the reality is that a lot of times  people are not even aware of the  symptoms that they're having half the  time right and we need to know what are  those symptoms somebody says just snap  out of it if you had a 103 degree fever  and you were suffering from the flu how  effective is that if your family members  said snap out it what are some of the  most significant stigmas that we that we  still have yes that we think that  somebody can change if they're drinking  if they're abusing substances we think  that this is some more  failure that they didn't try hard enough  right but you know one thing that's  interesting is when people die by  suicide if we were to think of it as  somebody dying by cancer how would it  change right like the person didn't want  to live and that leaves anytime you have  a mental illness or you have suicide it  affects not only a family a society  community people who are left behind  feel like failures people who are  experiencing it feel like failures  talking about it the way we are now that  helps break down the stigma but what  else can we do the science is clear the  health ramifications are clear how do we  break through to really change this this  this system I think it's so important  that people who are celebrated in our  community who are seen as very strong if  they're able to come out and share their  struggles as we have seen we see that  this can be a part of a normal person's  life if we're talking about one in five  people we look around the room right  here all of us are struggling with  something at some point in our lives so  normalizing it and people who are we put  on pedestals who are heroes they need to  come out we need to also be open when  we're struggling with something we need  to shed a positive light if you're going  to share with your friend the best  restaurant that you've been to why can't  you tell t